WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.435
Picture the bank's book of accounts: one page per account number, with a balance on each page.

2
00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:13.133
In a program, that book is a map, a collection that finds a value by its key.

3
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:17.099
Here the key is the account number, and the value is the balance.

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.290
On payday, eight tellers write in the book at once, each on its own thread.

5
00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:28.145
Back in the lesson on count++, a ConcurrentHashMap and one call, merge, kept every cent.

6
00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:30.141
We took the map's word for it.

7
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:33.966
So let's test three versions of the book.

8
00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:38.354
Eight tellers record 800,000 deposits across 1,000 accounts.

9
00:00:38.354 --> 00:00:39.963
First a plain HashMap.

10
00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:43.255
Then a ConcurrentHashMap, with get, then put.

11
00:00:43.255 --> 00:00:45.888
Then a ConcurrentHashMap with merge.

12
00:00:45.888 --> 00:00:48.009
Which ones count all 800,000?

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.803
The plain HashMap kept about a quarter of the deposits.

14
00:00:54.803 --> 00:01:00.059
It found 1,019 accounts in a bank that has 1,000, and it threw an exception.

15
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:00.587
Well.

16
00:01:00.587 --> 00:01:02.962
Nineteen accounts that nobody opened.

17
00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:07.967
That map was never built for more than one thread, and it never claimed to be.

18
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.050
The concurrent map with merge was exact.

19
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.540
But the concurrent map with get, then put, lost 2,232 deposits.

20
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.390
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

21
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.027
How can a thread-safe map lose money?

22
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:23.797
To answer it, let's look inside.

23
00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:30.669
Picture the bank's old card files: a cabinet of drawers, each holding the cards for some of the account numbers.

24
00:01:30.669 --> 00:01:33.368
A clerk who updates a card opens one drawer.

25
00:01:33.368 --> 00:01:36.927
Another clerk can work in another drawer at the same time.

26
00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:39.872
But two clerks can't work in one drawer at once.

27
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:40.899
Let's map it.

28
00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:44.341
The cabinet is the map, and each drawer is called a bin.

29
00:01:44.341 --> 00:01:48.275
A number computed from the key, its hash code, picks the drawer.

30
00:01:48.275 --> 00:01:53.929
And a clerk is a thread: while it holds a bin's lock, no other thread can write to that bin.

31
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.076
Two more rules.

32
00:01:55.076 --> 00:01:58.220
A write into an empty drawer takes no lock at all.

33
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.684
It uses compare and set, the clerk with the tally board from last time.

34
00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:04.004
And reads never lock.

35
00:02:04.004 --> 00:02:08.405
Anyone may read any card at any moment, even one a clerk is busy with.

36
00:02:08.405 --> 00:02:10.920
That's where the cabinet picture breaks.

37
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:13.482
Now the lost deposits.

38
00:02:13.482 --> 00:02:15.628
Say account 1042 holds 500.

39
00:02:15.628 --> 00:02:20.954
Teller A deposits 20, and teller B deposits 30, at the same moment.

40
00:02:20.954 --> 00:02:25.008
Each one calls get, adds its cents, then calls put.

41
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:27.399
A calls get, and reads 500.

42
00:02:27.399 --> 00:02:31.811
Reads take no lock, so B calls get, and also reads 500.

43
00:02:31.811 --> 00:02:33.495
A calls put with 520.

44
00:02:33.495 --> 00:02:36.784
It locks the drawer, writes, and lets go.

45
00:02:36.784 --> 00:02:38.388
Then B puts its sum.

46
00:02:38.388 --> 00:02:40.313
What does the card show?

47
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:43.597
530.

48
00:02:43.597 --> 00:02:45.230
A's 20 cents are gone.

49
00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.347
Each call held the drawer only for itself.

50
00:02:48.347 --> 00:02:54.804
Between A's get and A's put, nobody held anything, and B walked right through that gap.

51
00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:56.038
Now use merge.

52
00:02:56.038 --> 00:03:00.415
It reads, adds and writes, all inside one hold of the drawer.

53
00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:05.869
Its documentation says the entire method invocation is performed atomically.

54
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.345
A is in the middle of its merge, holding the drawer.

55
00:03:09.345 --> 00:03:11.779
B's merge arrives for the same account.

56
00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:12.840
What will B read?

57
00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:17.905
B waits at the drawer.

58
00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:20.260
A writes 520 and lets go.

59
00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:25.063
Then B takes the drawer, reads 520, and writes 550.

60
00:03:25.063 --> 00:03:26.569
Nothing is lost.

61
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.830
Meanwhile, teller C updates an account in a different drawer, and never meets A.

62
00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.470
Only writes to the same drawer take turns.

63
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.551
And teller D reads any card, with no lock at all.

64
00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:42.900
So the map gives you one call for each whole read, modify and write.

65
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.034
putIfAbsent inserts only if the key is missing.

66
00:03:46.034 --> 00:03:49.701
computeIfAbsent works out a value only if it's missing.

67
00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:53.368
compute and merge replace the value, using the old one.

68
00:03:53.368 --> 00:03:57.835
And replace with an expected value is compare and set, for one key.

69
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:00.172
One call is atomic for its key.

70
00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:01.802
Two calls are two steps.

71
00:04:01.802 --> 00:04:05.063
Do the whole read, modify and write in one call.

72
00:04:05.867 --> 00:04:06.829
One guardrail.

73
00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:11.913
The function you hand to merge or compute runs while the drawer is locked.

74
00:04:11.913 --> 00:04:13.081
So keep it short.

75
00:04:13.081 --> 00:04:21.050
If it called a slow service that takes 120 milliseconds, every write to that drawer would wait 120 milliseconds too.

76
00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.799
And never change the map from inside it.

77
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.645
Here it is in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

78
00:04:28.645 --> 00:04:33.485
A deposit is one merge: insert the cents, or add them to the old balance.

79
00:04:33.485 --> 00:04:38.060
Opening an account is one putIfAbsent, and only one caller gets true.

80
00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:41.282
So how can a thread-safe map lose money?

81
00:04:41.282 --> 00:04:44.664
Each call is safe, but the gap between two calls is not.

82
00:04:44.664 --> 00:04:49.435
The map locks one drawer per write, so tellers in different drawers never meet.

83
00:04:49.435 --> 00:04:53.421
Give each change to an account one call, and keep that call short.

84
00:04:53.633 --> 00:04:54.414
Quick check.

85
00:04:54.414 --> 00:04:57.409
Two tellers open the same new account at once.

86
00:04:57.409 --> 00:05:01.445
Each one checks that it's missing, then puts a page with zero.

87
00:05:01.445 --> 00:05:02.812
Can both of them put?
